he channel\ndocs/design/dao.stream.serve.md:937:- **No assumed graphs.** Who serves what to whom is the serve table and\ndocs/design/dao.stream.serve.md:948:concurrent writers and neither does this layer. Over an unordered channel\ndocs/design/dao.stream.serve.md:951:append does precede it at the source. An inbox pair preserves per-link\ndocs/design/dao.stream.serve.md:952:order only as far as its underlying channel does, and the proxy's policy\ndocs/design/dao.stream.serve.md:953:already reads that from the channel's declaration.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.serve.md:971:trial; a WebRTC DataChannel channel; authentication of peer ids and\ndocs/design/dao.stream.serve.md:974:nothing and this layer adds nothing); a lease over served entries and\ndocs/design/dao.stream.serve.md:975:inbox pairs (section 13); encryption of the channel (WSS is the host's;\ndocs/design/dao.stream.serve.md:976:UDP has none until a Noise-shaped channel exists, and until then UDP\ndocs/design/dao.stream.serve.md:989:  adds `identity -> {handle, surface}` to its serve table and writes a\ndocs/design/dao.stream.serve.md:1009:- **Lifetime.** The exporter must keep the entry served while the\ndocs/design/dao.stream.serve.md:1010:  migrated task may need it, and a meeting peer must keep the inbox pair\ndocs/design/dao.stream.serve.md:1011:  the route depends on. The correct home for both obligations is a lease\ndocs/design/dao.stream.serve.md:1012:  (`dao.lease.md`): the served entry and the inbox pair are grants, the\ndocs/design/dao.stream.serve.md:1014:  lapse unserves. Until that lease exists, migration acceptance pins\ndocs/design/dao.stream.serve.md:1025:   an in-process channel pair (two ring buffers as the two directions).\ndocs/design/dao.stream.serve.md:1040:   stream to a peer that only dials, through an inbox pair on a third\ndocs/design/dao.stream.serve.md:1042:   the third peer's serve table holds two ring buffers per pair and no\ndocs/design/dao.stream.serve.md:1045:   direct channel through a meeting stream's `:meet/seen` facts and a\ndocs/design/dao.stream.serve.md:1048:   symmetric-NAT simulator they fall to the inbox pair, reported as a\ndocs/design/dao.stream.serve.md:1051:   one channel.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.serve.md:1058:   duplicated on the outer inbox channel are absorbed by the inner layer\ndocs/design/dao.stream.serve.md:1066:    difference beyond which channels it has, plays the accepting,\ndocs/design/dao.stream.serve.md:1067:    dialing, inbox-holding and inbox-using positions in items 5 and 6.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.serve.md:1071:    channel is lost instead: the proxy deposits `:serve/detached`, `next`\ndocs/design/dao.stream.serve.md:1074:13. **Frame budget and oversize.** Over a channel with a small frame\ndocs/design/dao.stream.serve.md:1079:    crosses an unbounded channel intact.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.serve.md:1104:- **Lifetime of a served entry and of a meeting inbox pair after\ndocs/design/dao.stream.serve.md:1105:  migration.** Recommended: lease-governed through `dao.lease.md`, with\ndocs/design/dao.stream.serve.md:1151:  acceptance matrix's facade row adds the inbox-pair pinning; re-homing\ndocs/design/dao.stream.serve.md:1153:- `docs/design/dao.lease.md`: the carriage note that a remote holder\ndocs/design/dao.stream.serve.md:1155:  reason; served entries and inbox pairs are named as subjects a lease\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.lease.md | sed -n '1,15p;73,101p;110,138p;240,315p;330,390p' ; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.serve.md | sed -n '965,1018p;1098,1112p;1146,1158p' ; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '141,158p;538,550p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.lease.md | sed -n '1,15p;73,101p;110,138p;240,315p;330,390p' ; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.serve.md | sed -n '965,1018p;1098,1112p;1146,1158p' ; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '141,158p;538,550p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# DaoLease\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: proposed design target, derived from and subordinate to\n     4\t[`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md). This document is the operative contract:\n     5\tevery sentence below is a rule. Why the design is this shape is in\n     6\t[`dao.lease.rationale.md`](./dao.lease.rationale.md), which binds nothing.\n     7\t\n     8\tA lease is a grant that lapses unless renewed. It adds no operation to any\n     9\tcontract and no key to any DaoStream result map, leaving\n    10\t[`dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) unchanged by its existence.\n    11\t\n    12\t## Vocabulary\n    13\t\n    14\tFacts are plain data on ordinary streams, classified by two dispatch keys:\n    15\t`:dao.lease/status` for the negotiation, `:dao.lease/event` for evidence. A\n    73\t## Authority\n    74\t\n    75\t- **Only grantor-authored facts establish terms.** A holder cannot grant itself\n    76\t  tenure. A holder fact establishes no term; a delayed holder renewal is still\n    77\t  evidence and counts as such.\n    78\t- **A renewal counts only when its attributed author is the lease's holder.**\n    79\t  A renewal from any other author is a fact about that author, and silence is\n    80\t  measured as though it had not arrived.\n    81\t- **At most one of `:accepted` or `:rejected` answers a proposal.**\n    82\t  Renegotiating is a new proposal with a new proposal identity.\n    83\t- **A proposal creates no state.** Nothing exists until `:accepted`. A grantor\n    84\t  owes no answer, and a holder's wait on an unanswered proposal is bounded by\n    85\t  nothing in this vocabulary.\n    86\t- **A grantor may grant unsolicited**, with no proposal, in which case the\n    87\t  holder is the party the grant is delivered to.\n    88\t- **A `:policy` cause may not consist of declining to count evidence\n    89\t  received.** Eligible renewals are counted whether or not another condition\n    90\t  ends the lease.\n    91\t\n    92\t## Prohibitions\n    93\t\n    94\t- A lease may not extend retention, defer eviction, or gate history, and no\n    95\t  DaoStream operation may consult a lease.\n    96\t- No timer holds a callback and no notification marks a lapse.\n    97\t- No absolute time appears in any fact.\n    98\t- A reclaim frees the resource, never the record.\n    99\t- A holder renews from its own control flow. No machinery renews on its behalf.\n   100\t\n   101\t## Time\n   110\t\n   111\t## The judge\n   112\t\n   113\tThe judge is composed **inside the boundary that possesses the resource** and\n   114\treclaims by acting on what it itself holds; one that does not possess the\n   115\tresource is outside this contract. It concludes and reclaims in one\n   116\tinterpreter, which is not the forbidden collapse: it is the possessing host\n   117\tinterpreter performing its own act.\n   118\t\n   119\t**Ledger.** The judge keeps, privately, for each live lease:\n   120\t\n   121\t- its **terms** — subject, holder, duration, cap;\n   122\t- its **tenure start**, the reading the grant was seeded at, which no renewal\n   123\t  changes;\n   124\t- its **last relevant observation**, the reading of the grant or newest\n   125\t  eligible renewal, whichever is later;\n   126\t- its **evidence state** — `known`, or `unknown` with its resumed reading;\n   127\t- its **reclaim state** — `live`, or `pending` carrying the classified cause\n   128\t  and whether the reclaim succeeded.\n   129\t\n   130\tA lease **enters** the ledger when the grantor grants it, seeded from that act,\n   131\tand **leaves** once its reclaim has succeeded and its `:lapsed` record has been\n   132\tappended with `:dao.stream/ok`. Until both, it remains.\n   133\t\n   134\tThe ledger is not rebuildable from any stream: a composition either persists it,\n   135\tas a stream its owner writes and reads, or recovers by the rule under *Restart*.\n   136\t\n   137\t### The pass\n   138\t\n   240\t  none. One handed no resolver, or one incompatible with its medium, is refused\n   241\t  at assembly.\n   242\t- **A reclaim procedure per subject**, idempotent, reporting success.\n   243\t- **A stream the grantor writes grants and `:lapsed` to**, and the medium each\n   244\t  recipient reads its carried facts from.\n   245\t- **Media that either retain without eviction or declare evict-oldest**,\n   246\t  refused at assembly otherwise.\n   247\t- **For durable resources, three things this vocabulary does not supply**: a\n   248\t  judge whose durability matches the resource, a rule for which incarnation may\n   249\t  reclaim, and fencing from the resource. A composition that has not settled\n   250\t  all three keeps its leases process-scoped.\n   251\t\n   252\t## Sizing\n   253\t\n   254\tStated as relations; the values belong to compositions.\n   255\t\n   256\t- The granted duration exceeds twice the holder's renewal interval.\n   257\t- The judge's tolerance covers expected flight time and the rate skew between\n   258\t  its tick stream and the holder's.\n   259\t- A medium's retention window exceeds the judge's lag — cadence plus drain time.\n   260\t- A cap below the duration is permitted, and tenure then ends at the cap.\n   261\t- The holder's renewal interval plus one period of its tick stream is\n   262\t  strictly below half the granted duration: a renewal lands at the first\n   263\t  reading at or past the interval, up to one period late, so the\n   264\t  before-half guarantee is bought against interval plus period.\n   265\t\n   266\t## Limits\n   267\t\n   268\t- **A false lapse is possible.** Delay errs against the holder while a renewal\n   269\t  is unobserved and for it once observed, so a late-observed renewal from a\n   270\t  dead holder buys a fresh duration, and continuity is lost when none is\n   271\t  observed before a completed pass finds the interval past duration plus\n   272\t  tolerance. Partition length alone decides neither.\n   273\t- **`:dao.lease/max` binds within one ledger lifetime.**\n   274\t- **A medium that gaps more often than one duration** never completes an\n   275\t  observation window and leaks continuously while every component reports\n   276\t  healthy; per-attachment media are the isolation.\n   277\t- **Reclamation is bounded by** duration plus tolerance plus cadence plus the\n   278\t  reclaim's own cost, given an uninterrupted observation window.\n   279\t\n   280\t## Carriage\n   281\t\n   282\tProposals, grants, refusals, releases and renewals cross a boundary as ordinary\n   283\tpayload, carried by the composition to the medium the recipient reads. Carriage\n   284\tis delivery, not a second authoring.\n   285\t\n   286\t`:lapsed` does not cross; it is the grantor's record on the grantor's stream. A\n   287\tremote holder learns of a reclaim by observing it — for a served connection, an\n   288\tordinary `:ws/closed`. No close code is assigned here; distinguishing reclaim on\n   289\tthe wire belongs to `dao.stream.ws.md`'s deferred close-code design.\n   290\t\n   291\t## Out of scope\n   292\t\n   293\t- **Delegated renewal.** A renewal is evidence about its author.\n   294\t- **Transfer of a lease between holders.**\n   295\t- **Who may be granted anything at all**, and **what is leasable** — this\n   296\t  contract gates nothing, and the domain decides.\n   965\tidentity, the one-outstanding-append rule, anchor piggybacking, held\n   966\treads, candidate order and bounds, retry and reconnect policies, the\n   967\tmeeting and service conventions and their bounds.\n   968\t\n   969\t**Deliberately deferred:** UDP fragmentation (required before content is\n   970\tclaimed over UDP, section 6.3, but not designed here); parallel candidate\n   971\ttrial; a WebRTC DataChannel channel; authentication of peer ids and\n   972\tgating of a public meeting or service door (postage or capability, an\n   973\tinterpreter rule on that peer's own request medium; the contract gates\n   974\tnothing and this layer adds nothing); a lease over served entries and\n   975\tinbox pairs (section 13); encryption of the channel (WSS is the host's;\n   976\tUDP has none until a Noise-shaped channel exists, and until then UDP\n   977\tserving is for trusted networks, as the DHT already says); carrying\n   978\tcontent as CBOR byte strings instead of Base64 text.\n   979\t\n   980\t## 13. UCF integration\n   981\t\n   982\tWhat `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md` 7.4.3 and 7.5.3 should\n   983\tsay in place of the undefined \"standard stream-over-network facade\":\n   984\t\n   985\t- **Lift.** For each stream cell, the exporter checks: the handle's\n   986\t  declared surface covers what the cell needs; its cursors and outcome\n   987\t  maps are in the portable domain (`:dao.stream.serve/v1`, the one cursor\n   988\t  profile); and the exporter has at least one reachability candidate. It\n   989\t  adds `identity -> {handle, surface}` to its serve table and writes a\n   990\t  serve descriptor into the cell's `:yin.k/stream`. The cell's\n   991\t  `:yin.k/position` is the kept cursor, verbatim. Any check failing is\n   992\t  `:yin.k/unsatisfied` before minting, as the UCF already says. Exporter\n   993\t  and resumer are per-migration roles, a convention over symmetric peers.\n   994\t- **Lower.** The resumer `attach!`s each serve descriptor, receiving a\n   995\t  proxy. `next` at the kept cursor is the first demand; `blocked` until\n   996\t  served; the source's `gap` arrives verbatim. `:serve/disclaim` on open\n   997\t  is `not-found` and hence `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the identity. An\n   998\t  exhausted candidate list is the same.\n   999\t- **Put resume.** Through a proxy, `append!`'s `ok` is acceptance into\n  1000\t  the session's outbound path. A `:put` wait on a served stream therefore\n  1001\t  resumes when the source's outcome is observed on the proxy's event\n  1002\t  medium as `:serve/appended` with `ok`, not on the outbound `ok`. A\n  1003\t  `:serve/append-unknown` leaves the wait undischarged; the program or\n  1004\t  its composition decides, and nothing retries invisibly.\n  1005\t- **One cursor profile.** `:yin.k/cursor-profiles` names\n  1006\t  `:dao.stream.serve/v1` and nothing per transport. A per-transport\n  1007\t  profile would make the resumer know transports, which the proxy exists\n  1008\t  to prevent.\n  1009\t- **Lifetime.** The exporter must keep the entry served while the\n  1010\t  migrated task may need it, and a meeting peer must keep the inbox pair\n  1011\t  the route depends on. The correct home for both obligations is a lease\n  1012\t  (`dao.lease.md`): the served entry and the inbox pair are grants, the\n  1013\t  resumer renews, renewals count only on an observed source `ok`, and\n  1014\t  lapse unserves. Until that lease exists, migration acceptance pins\n  1015\t  both: the exporter retires nothing and M retires no pair while an\n  1016\t  acceptance is in progress, and a resumer that arrives after retirement\n  1017\t  gets `not-found`.\n  1018\t- **Pinning.** Serving from the exporter pins the exporter: the stream is\n  1098\t  Recommended: accept OD-3 (a) and (2) and OD-2 as drafted; accept OD-1\n  1099\t  with the write fallback corrected to \"effect unknown; no automatic\n  1100\t  retry\"; add the two definitions of section 4.1 (Reading, `blocked`;\n  1101\t  Cursors, anchors) and a declaration that a proxy is a handle of\n  1102\t  deferred remote observation. This design is not implementable without\n  1103\t  OD-3 and is dishonest without OD-2.\n  1104\t- **Lifetime of a served entry and of a meeting inbox pair after\n  1105\t  migration.** Recommended: lease-governed through `dao.lease.md`, with\n  1106\t  composition retirement as an explicitly weaker pre-grant stopgap and\n  1107\t  migration acceptance pinning both until then.\n  1108\t- **Rename `dao.stream.serving`.** Recommended: this layer is\n  1109\t  `dao.stream.serve`; today's forwarder-into-every-socket composition\n  1110\t  becomes `dao.stream.serving.copy` or another name that says copy or\n  1111\t  broadcast, and its docstring says it implements a client/server\n  1112\t  broadcast convention over the symmetric transport.\n  1146\t  \"server-side\" and \"serving host\" in Close to acceptor-side and the host\n  1147\t  holding the stream.\n  1148\t- `docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md`: 7.4.3 and 7.5.3\n  1149\t  cite this document for the facade; the put resume rule becomes section\n  1150\t  13's; `:yin.k/cursor-profiles` names `:dao.stream.serve/v1` only; the\n  1151\t  acceptance matrix's facade row adds the inbox-pair pinning; re-homing\n  1152\t  is stated out of scope.\n  1153\t- `docs/design/dao.lease.md`: the carriage note that a remote holder\n  1154\t  learns of a reclaim through `:ws/closed` becomes `:serve/close` with a\n  1155\t  reason; served entries and inbox pairs are named as subjects a lease\n  1156\t  may govern.\n  1157\t- `docs/design/daostream-udp-design.md`: marked superseded prior art; its\n  1158\t  DRDS reliability layer is not revived, and its NAT non-goal moves here.\n   141\t**Non-blocking polling.** The operation returns immediately with whatever is\n   142\ttrue now, including the answer \"nothing yet, ask again.\" Nothing parks and\n   143\tnothing is inverted: the caller keeps control and decides when to ask again.\n   144\tThis is POSIX `O_NONBLOCK` without `select`, and it is DaoStream's model.\n   145\t\n   146\t`next` is total and non-blocking (see Reading): every state of the stream\n   147\thas a defined data answer, and `:dao.stream/blocked` is `EAGAIN` — this\n   148\tposition holds nothing yet. `append!`'s `:dao.stream/full` is the same\n   149\tanswer for writes. No operation in this contract parks, and none takes a\n   150\tcallback; the Result Convention's \"never exceptions and never callbacks\" is\n   151\tthis model stated as a rule about results.\n   152\t\n   153\tThe rule generalizes past `next`: **no operation waits.** An operation that\n   154\tcannot yet know its answer returns the outcome true at the moment it is\n   155\tcalled, and the eventual answer arrives the way every other fact does — as\n   156\tdata, observed later, through an ordinary operation or on a stream. A\n   157\tDaoStream operation is never a request for something to happen and be\n   158\treported back. It is a question about now.\n   538\t`next` takes a handle and a cursor and returns an outcome immediately.\n   539\tIt is total and non-blocking: every state of the stream has a defined data\n   540\tanswer.\n   541\t\n   542\t| Outcome                       | Meaning                                                                       | Required keys                                                                           |\n   543\t|-------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n   544\t| `:dao.stream/ok`              | A value was observed.                                                         | `:dao.stream/value`, `:dao.stream/cursor` (successor)                                   |\n   545\t| `:dao.stream/blocked`         | Nothing at this position yet; retry later.                                    | —                                                                                       |\n   546\t| `:dao.stream/end`             | What this handle is on is closed and this position is past the retained tail. | —                                                                                       |\n   547\t| `:dao.stream/gap`             | This position was evicted.                                                    | `:dao.stream/cursor` (earliest retained position, or the tail when nothing is retained) |\n   548\t| `:dao.stream/cursor-mismatch` | The cursor was minted by a different logical stream.                          | —                                                                                       |\n   549\t| `:dao.stream/invalid-cursor`  | The value is not a cursor.                                                    | —                                                                                       |\n   550\t| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The transport failed to perform the read; nothing was observed.               | —                                                                                       |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-26 17:36:50 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-27 00:36:50 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\nCoding-Agent: codex\nSession-ID: 01a0debb-6229-79e1-890d-4d1e0b7d8565\n\n## L1. Which network resources need a lease\n\nDaoLease’s judge must run inside the boundary that holds the resource, and leases cannot gate stream history or be consulted by DaoStream operations. Reclaim the serving or routing resource itself; preserve its records. [dao.lease.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md:92) [dao.lease.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md:111)\n\n| Resource | Lease? Holder; grantor/judge; subject |\n|---|---|\n| Served table entry | **Yes** where a remote peer depends on its continued availability. Holder: attaching peer. Grantor/judge: peer holding the handle and table entry. Subject: `{stream-identity, entry-incarnation}`. Lapse removes the mapping, not the stream or its history. The earlier draft likewise proposed leasing served entries. [dao.stream.serve.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.serve.md:1009) |\n| Relay inbox pair | **Yes.** Each using peer holds a lease for its assigned inbox side; relay peer M grants and judges it. Subject: `{pair-id, side, incarnation}`. Reclaim that side’s inbox; retire the pair when neither side remains leased. |\n| Reflection link state | **No.** It belongs to the attaching peer and is freed on local `close!` or channel teardown; bound its outstanding requests and filed answers. |\n| UDP reassembly buffers | **No.** They are channel-internal bounded transient state; size and partial-message limits govern memory, and incomplete messages expire. |\n| NAT mapping and hole-punch state | **No.** Mapping expiry and punch attempts belong to the network/channel composition; they do not represent a separately granted application resource. |\n| Meeting-board address posting | **Yes, for an active route claim.** Holder: peer publishing its reflexive address. Grantor/judge: board-owning peer. Subject: `{peer-claim, socket, address, epoch}`. Lapse removes the active routing claim/index, not the append-only board history. |\n| Channel connection | **No by default.** Socket close and the channel’s liveness policy govern it; do not add a second lease solely to duplicate that lifecycle. If the composition chooses leases to reclaim half-open connections, the endpoint holding the socket grants, the remote attachment holds, and the subject is that connection incarnation. |\n\nThe grantor needs an attribution resolver for each holder; DaoStream itself supplies none. A channel attachment can attribute WebSocket facts, while UDP source-address attribution is not authentication and may change across NAT rebinding. [dao.lease.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md:232)\n\n## L2. The mechanism\n\n**RULING:** Carry lease facts over ordinary streams using the same reflection and mirror mechanism. Add no serve request or answer shape and no DaoStream operation. DaoLease explicitly makes its facts ordinary stream payloads and adds no operation or result key. [dao.lease.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md:8) [dao.lease.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md:280)\n\nFor each resource owner, composition exposes a stable, process-scoped lease-facts stream (or stream pair for holder-to-grantor evidence). Holders reach it as they reach other streams. Proposals, grants, refusals, releases, and renewals travel as ordinary payloads; the holder appends renewals under its own control flow. The lease-facts stream and basic reachability needed to access it are bootstrap composition resources, not themselves leased by the leases they carry. Grantor-local `:lapsed` records stay on the grantor’s stream. [dao.lease.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md:98) [dao.lease.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md:282)\n\nWhen a grantor lapses a served-entry lease, it removes the entry from the active table. A subsequent call for that identity returns a serve-defined `transport-error` outcome with a reason such as `:dao.serve/reclaimed`; the holder observes that ordinary result through its reflection. This uses the existing correlated outcome message, not a new frame. Relay-pair lapse closes or unserves the affected inbox; the user of that side observes channel closure or its reflected transport failure.\n\nLease expiry is not the same as channel loss. A dropped channel does not itself prove a lease lapsed; the grantor judges silence from lease evidence and its tick stream, with the specified tolerance and observation rules. Conversely, a lease may lapse while a shared channel remains open. [dao.lease.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md:137) [dao.lease.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md:266)\n\n**RISK:** The lapsed `transport-error` reason is a serve-layer extension to an outcome map. Ensure the mirror’s “missing identity” behavior distinguishes a revoked/expired entry from a never-known identity without inventing outcomes for operations whose contract does not permit them.\n\n## L3. The boundary\n\n| Change or rule | Belongs in | Ruling |\n|---|---|---|\n| `blocked` means nothing observable at this position *through this handle* yet | **dao.stream.md** | Handle-relative result semantics are part of the generic operation contract, not networking. |\n| Operations may initiate transport work but never wait for its answer | **dao.stream.md** | State this generically beside the no-waiting rule; the current text says no operation waits and answers what is true now. [dao.stream.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:141) |\n| `:dao.stream/retry?` for a `cursor` whose answer is pending | **dao.stream.serve.md** | Keep it a serve-specific optional result key, not a required DaoStream result key. Define its exact meaning and when it is valid there. |\n| `:dao.stream/reason :oversize` / revoked-or-missing reason | **dao.stream.serve.md** | These explain channel/serve-layer transport outcomes; lease itself adds no keys to DaoStream results. |\n| OD-2: append effect may be unknown; correlation/dedup rules | **dao.stream.md** for generic append semantics; **dao.stream.serve.md** for request IDs and retry policy | The core must define what `append!` can truthfully report; serve specifies how its remote path handles that. |\n| OD-3: cursor portability across serialization | **dao.stream.md** | Cross-host cursor validity is a generic cursor promise if a reflection is to be another handle on the same logical stream. The current text leaves serialization transport-owned and TBD. [dao.stream.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:521) |\n\n**Q1, reconsidered:** The earlier objection does not stand once the contract explicitly permits an operation to initiate work and defines `blocked` as handle-relative. A reflection may return `blocked` while it has not yet received the source value, then return the filed source outcome on a later call; that operation does not wait. The current text’s “nothing at this position yet” gloss needs the handle-relative clarification, and `cursor` still needs a serve-specific retry signal because `cursor` has no `blocked` outcome. [dao.stream.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:153) [dao.stream.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:542)\n\n**RISK:** `blocked` must mean no value is observable through this reflection yet, not assert that the source itself returned `blocked`. Preserve this distinction when documenting filed source outcomes.\n\n## L4. Delta and readiness\n\n**RULING: READY TO SPECIFY**, with lease integration as a composition over exposed lease streams, not a serve protocol feature.\n\n**WHY:** Add optional leases to active served-entry and relay-inbox lifetimes, and to expiring rendezvous claims. Leave mirror dispatch stateless: its table reflects current composition state, while a separate owner-side judge removes resources on successful reclaim. Reflection state remains local and channel-bounded. The fable design’s request, outcome, ID, and UDP fragment shapes are unchanged.\n\n**DELTA (under 400 words):** Add a process-scoped lease-facts stream to the owner’s composition and expose it through the same table/reflection path. The holder observes a grant before relying on the resource and renews by appending lease facts; the grantor judges renewals and removes the active served-entry mapping or relay inbox side on reclaim. A revoked identity remains in lease history but is no longer available for new operations. The reflection learns this through a serve-defined transport-error reason on its ordinary result path; no lease frame is added. Lapsed records remain grantor-local, consistent with DaoLease carriage, where remote holders learn reclaim from the resource’s observable closure rather than receiving the lapsed fact itself. [dao.lease.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md:280)\n\nFor relays, use a separately attributable lease per participant/side so one peer’s lapse does not silently revoke the other’s active inbox. Meeting-board address claims can be leased as active routing state; their historical payloads remain readable, and interpreters ignore expired claims. No lease is needed for the reflection’s local request state, bounded UDP fragments, punch attempts, or channel lifecycle.\n\n**RISK / OWNER-VISIBLE:** Leases do not guarantee continuity during partitions: false lapse is possible, and a partition alone decides neither survival nor expiry. [dao.lease.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md:266) An expired served entry can become unavailable while its underlying stream and retained history remain intact. Direct NAT traversal is still impossible in some cases without a mutually reachable relay. [dao.lease.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md:268)\n\nDaoLease is marked “proposed design target,” though its text calls itself the operative contract. That is **not a blocker to writing the serve specification against it**, but DaoLease must be accepted as the governing target before claiming the lifetime integration is an implemented, normative system rule. [dao.lease.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md:3)"}}
